<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Othello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1603</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Othello is a tragedy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>, written around 1603 and first published in 1622. It tells of a Moorish general in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> who is driven by his scheming ensign Iago to murder his wife, Desdemona. Scholars have linked the play's Moorish hero to English interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, including the visit of the Moroccan embassy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-wahid-ben-messaoud">Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1600.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/othello.jpg" alt="Othello" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Théodore Chassériau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Othello_et_Desd%C3%A9mone_%C3%A0_Venise_-_Th%C3%A9odore_Chass%C3%A9riau_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_Peintures_RF_3897.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Shakespeare's main source was the seventh story of the third decade of Gli Hecatommithi, a collection of one hundred novellas published in 1565 by Cinthio, whose plot he borrowed and substantially reworked. In Cinthio only Disdemona is named, a name that means ill-omened in Italian; the others are called the Moor, the Ensign and the Corporal. There the Ensign falls in love with Disdemona, turns to hatred when she is indifferent, and persuades the Moor of her unfaithfulness. It cannot be later than 1604, since a performance that year is recorded in the accounts of Sir Edmund Tilney, the Master of the Revels, and it cannot be earlier than 1601, when Holland's translation of Pliny's Natural History, one of its sources, was published.</p><p>The play was not published in Shakespeare's lifetime. It survives in a quarto of 1622 and, a year later, in the First Folio. The Folio has about 160 lines that the quarto lacks, among them Desdemona's Willow Song.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The play is set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. In the first act Roderigo, a wealthy and dissolute gentleman, complains to his friend Iago, an ensign, of the secret marriage of Desdemona, the daughter of the senator Brabantio, to Othello, a Moorish general in the Venetian army. In Cyprus, Iago has Cassio, Othello's lieutenant, made drunk, so that Othello strips him of his rank, and then persuades Othello to suspect Cassio and Desdemona.</p><p>The handkerchief that Othello gave Desdemona is found by Iago's wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>, and passed to Iago, who plants it in Cassio's lodgings. Othello, convinced of his wife's unfaithfulness, vows with Iago to kill her and Cassio. The action is continuous from the wedding night, apart from the voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the time spent on the island is estimated at about 33 hours, which leaves no time for the supposed affair. The play's earliest critics already noted its reliance on the handkerchief; Thomas Rymer, in 1693, suggested it be called the Tragedy of the Handkerchief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The King's Men performed Othello at the Globe, and at Court on 1 November 1604 and again in 1612 and 1613 for the wedding of Princess Elizabeth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V of the Palatinate</a>. The first professional appearance of a woman on the English stage was as Desdemona on 8 December 1660. Originally performed by white actors in dark makeup, the title role began to be played by black actors in the 19th century; the first known was James Hewlett in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1822, and Ira Aldridge played it at the Royalty Theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1825.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Othello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dealings of al-Mansur with Elizabeth I were part of the early history of England’s relations with the Islamic world, and his envoy’s visit to London has been linked to the context of Shakespeare’s <b>Othello</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>On the English stage he appears in the prologue of Christopher Marlowe's The Jew of Malta, and Shakespeare's <b>Othello</b>, Macbeth and Richard III have characters of the type called the stage Machiavel.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-prince">The Prince · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Edwin <b>Othello</b> Excell's arrangement, published between 1900 and 1910, became the standard form in American churches.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace</a></li><li><blockquote>A woman, either Margaret Hughes or Anne Marshall, appeared on an English public stage for the first time, as Desdemona in Shakespeare's <b>Othello</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The King's Men performed Shakespeare's <b>Othello</b> in the Banqueting House at Whitehall, the earliest recorded performance of the play.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
